---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi4_72",
  "series": "Band VI 4 (1677 - 1690)",
  "seq": 72,
  "text": "[[221.16]] <b>60.  Calculus consequentiarum [April 1679]</b>\n [[221.17]] <tab/>[April 1679]\n[[222.7]] <tit2><b>Calculus consequentiarum</b> </tit2> </p>\n<p> [[222.8]] Duo sunt quae in omni argumentatione dijudicari debent: Forma, nimirum et Materia.\n[[222.9]] Contingere enim potest ut argumentum aliquando succedat in certa materia, quod aliis\n[[222.10]] omnibus exemplis ejusdem formae applicari non potest. Exempli causa si ita ratiocinemur: </p>\n<p> [[222.12]] <i>Omne Triangulum est trilaterum</i> </p>\n<p> [[222.13]] <i>Quoddam Triangulum non est aequilaterum</i> </p>\n<p> [[222.14]] <i>Ergo quoddam Aequilaterum non est Trilaterum</i>.\n [[222.15]] Conclusio bona est sed vi materiae non formae, nam exempla similis formae afferri\n[[222.16]] possunt, quae non succedunt, exempli causa: </p>\n<p> [[222.17]] <i>Omne metallum est minerale</i> </p>\n<p> [[222.18]] <i>Quoddam metallum non est aurum</i> </p>\n<p> [[222.19]] <i>Ergo quoddam aurum non est minerale</i>. </p>\n<p> [[222.20]] Itaque et calculus qui Materiam tangit a calculo formali separari potest. Cum enim\n[[222.21]] invenissem cuilibet sive Termino sive notioni, suum ascribi posse numerum characteristicum,\n[[222.22]] cujus interventu idem futurum est calculare et ratiocinari; et vero ob mirificam\n[[222.23]] rerum complicationem, nondum veros numeros characteristicos exhibere possim, antequam\n[[222.24]] summa plerarumque rerum capita in ordinem redegero; consideravi consequentiarum\n[[222.25]] formam nihilominus calculo comprehendi ac numeris demonstrari posse fictitiis, qui\n[[222.26]] loco verorum numerorum characteristicorum interim adhiberentur. Quod ita patefaciam. </p>\n<p> [[222.27]] In omni propositione Categorica (nam ex his caeteras dijudicari posse alias ostendam,\n[[222.28]] paucis in calculo mutatis[)] duo sunt Termini subjectum et praedicatum; quibus\n[[222.29]] accedunt: copula (<i>est</i>), affirmatio vel negatio, seu qualitas, et denique signum id est <i>omnis</i>\n[[223.1]] vel <i>quidam</i> seu quantitas. Exempli gratia in hac: <i>Pius est felix</i>, <i>pius</i> et <i>felix</i> sunt termini,\n[[223.2]] ex quibus <i>pius</i> est subjectum, <i>felix</i> praedicatum; <i>est</i>, copula. <b>Qualitas Propositionis</b>\n[[223.3]] est Affirmatio vel Negatio. Ita haec propositio: <i>pius est felix</i>, affirmat; illa vero: <i>sceleratus\n[[223.4]] non est felix</i>, negat. <b>Quantitas propositionis</b> est universalitas vel particularitas.\n[[223.5]] Ut cum dico: <i>Omnis pius est felix</i>, vel si dicam <i>nullus sceleratus est felix</i>; sunt\n[[223.6]] propositiones universales, illa universalis affirmativa, haec negativa. Sed si dicam: <i>quidam\n[[223.7]] sceleratus est fortunatus</i>, <i>quidam pius non est fortunatus</i>, Propositiones sunt particulares,\n[[223.8]] illa affirmativa, haec negativa. </p>\n<p> [[223.9]] In omni propositione praedicatum inesse dicitur subjecto, seu praedicati notio in\n[[223.10]] subjecti notione involvitur. Nam in propositione Universali affirmativa cum dico: <i>Omnis\n[[223.11]] homo est animal</i>; hoc volo: <i>animalis</i> conceptum involvi in <i>hominis</i> conceptu (nam\n[[223.12]] <i>hominis</i> conceptus est, <i>esse animal rationale</i>). Et cum dico <i>Omnis pius est felix</i>, significo\n[[223.13]] eum qui intelligat naturam pietatis, etiam intellecturum in ea felicitatem veram contineri.\n[[223.14]] Itaque in propositione universali affirmativa manifestum est praedicatum in subjecto per\n[[223.15]] se spectato contineri. Sed si propositio sit particularis affirmativa, tunc praedicatum non\n[[223.16]] continetur in subjecti notione per se spectata, sed in subjecti notione cum aliquo addito\n[[223.17]] sumta; id est in aliqua subjecti specie. Fit enim speciei notio ex notione generis, cum\n[[223.18]] addita aliqua differentia. </p>\n<p> [[223.19]] Similiter in Propositione Negativa cum negamus praedicatum hoc modo quo dixi\n[[223.20]] subjecto inesse; eo ipso affirmamus negationem praedicati sive terminum praedicato\n[[223.21]] contradictorium subjecto inesse. Ut cum dico: <i>Nullus sceleratus est felix</i>: idem est ac si\n[[223.22]] dicerem: <i>Omnis sceleratus est non-felix</i>, seu non-felicitatem scelerato inesse. Et cum\n[[223.23]] dico: [<i>quidam</i>] <i>pius est non-fortunatus</i>, hoc volo: <greek>tò</greek> <i>non-fortunatum</i> inesse cuidam\n[[223.24]] speciei seu exemplo <i>pii</i>. </p>\n<p> [[223.25]] Considerandum porro est, omnem notionem compositam, constare ex pluribus aliis\n[[223.26]] notionibus, interdum positivis, interdum et negativis. Exempli gratia cum dico: <i>numerus\n[[223.27]] primitivus</i>, intelligo hoc: <i>numerus</i> <i>non-divisibilis per</i> <i>majorem unitate</i>. </p>\n<p> [[224.1]] Ideo ut generaliter procedamus: quamlibet notionem exprimemus duobus Numeris\n[[224.2]] characteristicis, uno cum nota + seu <i>plus</i>, altero cum nota ─ seu <i>minus</i>. Exempli gratia: </p>\n<p> [[224.3]] <i>Primitivus</i> est <tab/><i>numerus <tab/>indivisibilis</i>.\n [[224.4]] <tab/> +22 <tab/> ─17\n [[224.5]] Considerandum etiam est Terminos omnes negativos, hanc habere proprietatem, ut\n[[224.6]] quando positivi se habent ut genus et species, contra negativi eorum se habeant inverso\n[[224.7]] modo, ut species et genus. Exempli gratia <i>Corpus</i> est genus, <i>Animal</i> est species; latius\n[[224.8]] enim patet <i>corpus</i> quam <i>animal</i>, quia <i>corpus</i> continet <i>animalia</i> et <i>plantas</i> aliaque. Sed\n[[224.9]] contra <i>non-animal</i> est latius quam <i>non-corpus</i>. Omnia enim non-corpora sunt etiam\n[[224.10]] non-animalia; sed non contra; dantur enim non-animalia, quae tamen non sunt non-corpora,\n[[224.11]] verbi gratia plantae. Itaque quemadmodum plura dantur corpora quam animalia; ita\n[[224.12]] contra plura dantur non-animalia quam non-corpora. </p>\n<p> [[224.13]] His ita intellectis possumus vera ponere fundamenta calculi nostri. Nimirum omnis\n[[224.14]] notionis positivae (negativae) numerum characteristicum positivum (negativum) seu\n[[224.15]] nota + (vel ─) affectum conflabimus ex multiplicatione in se invicem omnium numerorum\n[[224.16]] characteristicorum earum notionum positivarum (negativarum), ex quibus ipsius\n[[224.17]] termini positiva (negativa) notio componitur. </p>\n<p> [[224.18]] ^&.bb <Leibniz special character>Numerus characterist<tab/>icus <tab/> <i>animal</i> <tab/> <i>homo</i> <tab/> <i>rationale</i> ^&.b</i> <Leibniz special character> Ita sit <tab/> <i>animal</i> <tab/> <i>rationale</i>\n [[224.19]] <tab/> +13 ─5 <tab/> +8 ─7\n [[224.20]] fiet Termini hujus: <tab/> <i>homo</i> <Fn><i>Am Rande</i>: <tab/> 13 <tab/> 8 <tab/>^#1+ 104 ^#1-</Fn>\n [[224.21]] Numerus characteristicus <tab/>+13 , 8 ─5 , 7\n [[224.22]] <tab/>sive <tab/> +104 ─35.\n [[225.1]] Hoc unum tantum in ista Numerorum efformatione cavendum est ne idem aliquis numerus\n[[225.2]] in positivis et in negativis contineatur, id est ne positivus et negativus numerus dividi\n[[225.3]] possint per unum eundemque numerum, seu habeant communem divisorem. Nam si sic\n[[225.4]] scripsissemus:\n [[225.5]] <tab/> <i>animal</i> <tab/> <i>rationale</i>\n [[225.6]] <tab/> +13 ─5 <tab/>+10 ─7\n [[225.7]] <tab/> <i>homo</i>\n [[225.8]] <tab/> +130 ─35\n [[225.9]] scripsissemus absurdum. Nam notio quae significatur per +5, contradictoria est ejus quae\n[[225.10]] significatur per ─5. Itaque cum in <i>rationalis</i> notione positiva 10 contineatur 5 (nam 10\n[[225.11]] dividi potest per 5 seu 10 fit ex multiplicatione 5 in 2), seu cum in <i>rationali</i> ponatur 5; in\n[[225.12]] <i>animali</i> autem contra negetur 5, seu contineatur contradictorium ipsius 5, sequetur <i>animal</i>\n[[225.13]] et <i>rationale</i> esse incompatibilia, adeoque <i>hominem</i> ex ipsis compositum implicare\n[[225.14]] contradictionem quoniam ita tam positivus ejus numerus +130, quam negativus dividi\n[[225.15]] potest per 5, quod cum falsum sit consequens est absurdam fore hanc exprimendi rationem,\n[[225.16]] adeoque semper cavendum esse, ne numerus positivus et negativus habeant eundem\n[[225.17]] divisorem. </p>\n<p> [[225.18]] Intellectis jam terminis sigillatim sumtis, videamus et quomodo conjungi possint;\n[[225.19]] seu quomodo propositionum quantitas, qualitas, et <b>veritas</b> (in quantum id fieri potest\n[[225.20]] ratione, seu numeris characteristicis) dignoscatur. Nimirum generaliter omnis propositio\n[[225.21]] <b>falsa</b> quae cognosci potest sola vi rationis, seu quae in terminis implicat; haec est: in qua\n[[225.22]] subjectum et praedicatum continent notiones incompatibiles, sive in qua duo quidam\n[[225.23]] numeri characteristici diversorum terminorum (subjecti unum praedicati alterum) diversarumque\n[[225.24]] notarum (unum cum nota +, alterum cum nota ─) habent communem divisorem.\n[[225.25]] Exempli causa sit propositio:\n [[226.1]] ^&.bb <Leibniz special character><i>Quidam</i> <tab/><i>fortunatus</i> <tab/><i>est</i> <tab/><i>animal</i> <tab/><i>rationale</i>^&.b</i> <Leibniz special character> <tab/> <i>pius</i> <tab/><i>est</i> <tab/> <i>miser</i>\n [[226.2]] <tab/> +10 ─3 <tab/> +14 ─5\n [[226.3]] Patet terminos +10 (id est +<i>bis</i> 5) et ─5 esse incompatibiles, significant enim contradictoria,\n[[226.4]] ac proinde statim ex numeris ipsorum characteristicis patet propositionem cui hi\n[[226.5]] numeri conveniunt esse <b>falsam</b> in terminis, et contradictoriam ejus esse ex terminis\n[[226.6]] <b>veram.</b> </p>\n<p> [[226.7]] Porro antequam specialibus Propositionum formis secundum quantitatem et qualitatem\n[[226.8]] suos numeros characteristicos accommodemus, illud in genere repetendum est,\n[[226.9]] quod supra diximus, Notionem praedicati semper inesse subjecto aut ejus speciei. Hoc\n[[226.10]] jam in Numeros characteristicos ita transferamus: </p>\n<p> [[226.11]] Esto propositio Universalis affirmativa:\n [[226.12]] <i>Omnis</i> <tab/> <i>sapiens</i> <tab/><i>est</i> <tab/> <i>pius</i>\n [[226.13]] <tab/> +70 ─33 <tab/> +10 ─3\n [[226.14]] Patet praedicatum inesse debere notioni subjecti per se sumtae, quia in omni casu inest,\n[[226.15]] adeoque numeros characteristicos subjecti dividi posse per numeros characteristicos\n[[226.16]] praedicati ejusdem notae, ut: +70 per +10, et ─33 per ─3.\n [[226.17]] Similiter: <Fn><i>Am Rande</i>: <i>Omne animal non-homo est corpus sentiens non rationale.</i> <i>Omnis</i> <i>quinarius non binarius</i> <i>Omnis quaternarius non-^#6+major denario^#6- est figuratus non-quadratoquadratus.</i></Fn>\n [[226.18]] <i>Omnis</i> <tab/> <i>homo</i> <tab/><i>est</i> <tab/> <i>animal</i> <tab/> <i>rationale</i>\n [[226.19]] <tab/> +130 ─35 <tab/> +13 ─5 <tab/> +10 ─7\n [[226.20]] Patet +130 dividi posse per +13 et per +10; et ─35 dividi posse per ─5 et ─7. </p>\n<p> [[227.1]] In Propositione autem Affirmativa particulari, quemadmodum supra diximus, sufficit\n[[227.2]] notionem praedicati inesse notioni subjecti, additamento aliquo auctae, seu praedicatum\n[[227.3]] inesse speciei subjecti, id est characteristicos numeros subjecti multiplicatos per\n[[227.4]] alios numeros reddi posse divisibiles per characteristicos numeros praedicati. Cumque id\n[[227.5]] semper possit fieri, quilibet enim numerus multiplicando reddi potest per alium numerum\n[[227.6]] quemlibet divisibilis; hinc patet propositionem particularem affirmativam semper habere\n[[227.7]] locum; nisi aliqua ex supra dicto capite incompatibilitas seu pugna oriatur. Exempli\n[[227.8]] causa:\n [[227.9]] <i>Quidam</i> <tab/><i>fortunatus</i> <tab/><i>est</i> <tab/> <i>miser</i>\n [[227.10]] <tab/> +11 ─9 <tab/> +5 ─14\n [[227.11]] Patet effici posse, ut <i>miseria</i> sit in aliqua <i>fortunati</i> specie; in eo scilicet qui fortuita\n[[227.12]] aeternis praefert. Jam species aliqua <i>fortunati</i> habet notionem compositam ex notione\n[[227.13]] <i>fortunati</i> tanquam genere, et notione differentiae hujus fortunati ab alio qui miser non\n[[227.14]] erit, haec differentia sit +15 ─28, fiet\n [[227.15]] <i>quidam fortunatus</i>\n [[227.16]] <picture>9029</picture>\n [[227.17]] +15 , 11 ─28 , 9\n [[227.18]] Jam 15 , 11 dividi potest per [5] et 28 , 9 per 14. Itaque patet effici posse, ut praedicatum\n[[227.19]] insit speciei subjecti. </p>\n<p> [[227.20]] Eadem mutatis mutandis etiam ad propositiones negativas transferri possunt. Exempli\n[[227.21]] gratia: [<i>bricht ab</i>] </p>\n",
  "number": "60.",
  "title": "Calculus consequentiarum",
  "date": "[April 1679]"
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:60. Calculus consequentiarum [April 1679] [April 1679] Calculus consequentiarum Duo sunt quae in omni argumentatione dijudicari debent: Forma, nimirum et Materia. Contingere enim potest ut argumentum aliquando succedat in certa materia, quod aliis omnibus exemplis ejusdem formae applicari non potest...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>